{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% block content %}
<p>Most chatbots remember by resending the whole conversation. This game deliberately
<strong>doesn't</strong>. Each turn is a fresh agent loop with no
<code>previous_response_id</code>. Instead, memory lives in four places, all inside the
saved world:</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Memory</th><th>Where</th><th>Shown to the model</th></tr>
  <tr><td>What exists right now</td><td>places, things, beings, player status</td><td>Current place, inventory, nearby beings</td></tr>
  <tr><td>What happened recently</td><td><code>journal</code></td><td>Last 8 entries</td></tr>
  <tr><td>What must stay true</td><td><code>facts</code></td><td>Relevant ones (keyword search)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>The long story</td><td><code>story_so_far</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
  <tr><td>What NPCs think of you</td><td><code>Being.memory</code>, <code>disposition</code></td><td>For beings in the room</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why not chat history?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>It grows forever.</strong> After 200 turns you'd be sending a novel every turn.</li>
  <li><strong>It goes stale.</strong> Turn 3 says "the door is locked"; turn 40 the door was
      burned down. History contains both; the world contains only the truth.</li>
  <li><strong><code>/undo</code> becomes trivial.</strong> Restore the world, done. With chat
      history you'd also have to rewind the conversation.</li>
  <li><strong>It forces good persistence.</strong> If the model can only remember what it
      saved, it learns (via the rules) to save.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The chronicler</h2>
<p>The journal window only shows the last few turns. Every 10 turns a separate LLM call
condenses the older journal into <code>story_so_far</code> and extracts lasting facts.
It reasons in a scratchpad first, then answers with JSON:</p>
{{ snippet("chronicler.py", "chronicle") }}
{{ snippet("prompts/chronicle_system.jinja") }}

<div class="box lib"><code>response.scratchpad_json()</code> returns
<code>(scratchpad_text, data)</code>: everything before the last <code>```json</code> block,
and that block parsed. Letting the model "think out loud" before answering often improves
the answer, and the scratchpad is great for debugging.</div>

<div class="box try">Play 10 turns, then check <code>/journal</code> (the "Story so far" line)
and the "Chronicle" section in the debug drawer.</div>

<div class="box exercise">NPCs only keep their last 10 memories. Make the chronicler also
